Syntax Basics.Comments and Formatting:
## Comments

```rust
// A single-line comment

/*
   A multi-line block comment
   spanning several lines
*/

/// A documentation comment (three slashes) — documents the item below it,
/// and appears in `cargo doc` generated documentation.
fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b
}

//! A module/crate-level doc comment (slash-bang) — placed at the TOP of a
//! file to document the module or crate itself, not the item below it.
```

Doc comments (`///` and `//!`) are covered fully in [Documentation Tests](/rust/documentation-tests/) — they support Markdown and their code examples are actually compiled and run by `cargo test`.

## Formatting conventions

Rust has one official formatter, `rustfmt`, and the community overwhelmingly uses its default style — so unlike languages with many competing style guides, "idiomatic Rust formatting" basically just means "whatever `cargo fmt` outputs." Key conventions:

- **4-space indentation**, never tabs.
- **snake_case** for variables, functions, and modules: `let user_name`, `fn calculate_total()`.
- **PascalCase** for types: structs, enums, traits: `struct UserAccount`, `enum HttpMethod`.
- **SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE** for constants: `const MAX_RETRIES: u32 = 5;`.
- Opening braces on the same line as the declaration:

```rust
fn example() {
    if true {
        // ...
    }
}
```

You don't need to memorize these — just run `cargo fmt` and it handles all of it automatically. See [clippy and rustfmt](/rust/clippy-and-rustfmt/) for configuration options.

Next: [Variables and Mutability](/rust/variables-and-mutability/).

Syntax Basics.Variables and Mutability:
In Rust, variables are **immutable by default**. This is one of the first things that surprises newcomers from most other languages.

```rust
fn main() {
    let x = 5;
    println!("x is {x}");

    x = 6; // ERROR: cannot assign twice to immutable variable `x`
}
```

The compiler will refuse to compile this and explain exactly why. To make a variable mutable, add `mut`:

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut x = 5;
    println!("x is {x}");

    x = 6; // fine
    println!("x is now {x}");
}
```

## Why immutable by default?

This isn't arbitrary — it's a deliberate design choice that pays off constantly in larger programs: when you read `let x = ...` you know, guaranteed by the compiler, that `x` never changes for the rest of its scope. When you see `let mut x`, that's a visible signal to you (and anyone reading your code) that this value *will* change somewhere below. This eliminates an entire category of "wait, who changed this variable?" bugs.

## Type inference

You don't have to annotate types explicitly — Rust infers them from context:

```rust
let x = 5;          // inferred as i32 (the default integer type)
let y = 5.0;         // inferred as f64 (the default float type)
let name = "Ferris"; // inferred as &str
```

You *can* annotate explicitly when needed or wanted, using a colon:

```rust
let x: i32 = 5;
let y: f64 = 5.0;
let count: u32 = 0;
```

Explicit annotations are required when the compiler genuinely can't infer the type — for example, when parsing a string and the target type is ambiguous:

```rust
let guess: u32 = "42".parse().expect("not a number");
```

## Declaring without initializing

You can declare a variable without a value, as long as you assign it before it's used:

```rust
let x: i32;
// ... some conditional logic ...
x = 5; // must happen before x is ever read
println!("{x}");
```

The compiler tracks this and will refuse to compile code that might read an uninitialized variable — this is different from C, where reading uninitialized memory is legal (and a classic source of bugs).

Next: [Constants and Statics](/rust/constants-and-statics/).

Syntax Basics.Expressions vs Statements:
This distinction trips up almost every newcomer at least once, and understanding it early makes a lot of Rust syntax click into place — including why functions often have no `return` keyword.

## The rule

- A **statement** performs an action and does *not* return a value.
- An **expression** evaluates to a value.

```rust
let y = 6; // this whole line is a statement (it doesn't produce a value itself)

let x = (let y = 6); // ERROR — `let y = 6` is a statement, not an expression,
                       // so it has no value to assign to x
```

That second example is actually invalid Rust and won't compile — it's included specifically to show the boundary. This is different from C or Ruby, where assignment *is* an expression that returns a value.

## Blocks are expressions

A `{ ... }` block is itself an expression, and it evaluates to whatever its **last line is, if that last line has no semicolon**:

```rust
fn main() {
    let y = {
        let x = 3;
        x + 1   // no semicolon — this is the block's value
    };

    println!("{y}"); // 4
}
```

Adding a semicolon after `x + 1` would turn it into a statement, and the block would evaluate to `()` (unit) instead — this is a very common beginner mistake, and the compiler error you get ("expected type, found `()`" or similar) is a strong signal to check for a stray trailing semicolon.

## This is why functions don't need `return`

```rust
fn add_one(x: i32) -> i32 {
    x + 1  // no semicolon = this is the return value
}
```

This works because the function body is a block, and a block's value (its final semicolon-less expression) becomes the value of the whole block — and in a function, that's the return value. You *can* use an explicit `return` keyword too, and it's normal to do so for early returns:

```rust
fn absolute_value(x: i32) -> i32 {
    if x < 0 {
        return -x; // early return, needs the keyword since it's not the last line
    }
    x // final expression, still no semicolon or return needed
}
```

## if as an expression

Because blocks are expressions, `if`/`else` is too:

```rust
let condition = true;
let number = if condition { 5 } else { 6 };
```

Both branches must produce the same type. This is covered further in [If and Else](/rust/if-and-else/) and [Match Expressions](/rust/match-expressions/), where the same "it's an expression" idea extends to `match`.

Next: [Shadowing](/rust/shadowing/).

Ownership and Memory.Ownership Rules:
Let's see the three rules from the previous page in real code.

```rust
fn main() {
    {
        let s = String::from("hello"); // s is the owner of this String's heap memory
        println!("{s}");
    } // this scope ends here — s goes out of scope, and Rust automatically
      // calls the equivalent of `drop(s)`, freeing the heap memory. This happens
      // for you, with no garbage collector and no manual `free()` call.

    // println!("{s}"); // ERROR if uncommented: s is not defined here, it's out of scope
}
```

`String::from("hello")` allocates memory on the heap to hold the text (unlike a `&str` literal, which is baked directly into the compiled binary — covered fully in [String vs str](/rust/string-vs-str/)). The variable `s` **owns** that heap allocation. When `s`'s scope ends (the closing `}`), Rust calls a special function called `drop` automatically, which frees the memory. This is deterministic and immediate — unlike garbage collection, you know exactly when it happens: at the closing brace.

## What "single owner" prevents

Consider what happens when you assign one variable to another:

```rust
fn main() {
    let s1 = String::from("hello");
    let s2 = s1; // ownership MOVES from s1 to s2

    println!("{s1}"); // ERROR: value borrowed here after move
}
```

This is the part that surprises everyone coming from other languages. In Python, JavaScript, or Java, `let s2 = s1` would either copy the value or create a second reference to the same object, and both `s1` and `s2` would remain valid afterward. In Rust, this line **moves** ownership from `s1` to `s2` — and then `s1` is no longer valid. The compiler enforces this at compile time; the code above simply won't compile, with an error message explaining exactly what happened and where.

This is explored in full on the next page, [Move Semantics](/rust/move-semantics/) — it's the single most distinctive Rust behavior and deserves its own page with several worked examples.

## Ownership and function calls

Passing a value to a function also transfers ownership, following the exact same rule:

```rust
fn main() {
    let s = String::from("hello");
    takes_ownership(s); // s's value moves into the function

    // println!("{s}"); // ERROR: s was moved and is no longer valid here
}

fn takes_ownership(some_string: String) {
    println!("{some_string}");
} // some_string goes out of scope here, and is dropped
```

This might feel restrictive right now — and it would be, if this were the whole story. It isn't: the next few pages cover **references and borrowing**, which let you give a function *temporary access* to a value without transferring ownership at all. That's the tool you'll actually reach for most of the time in real code; moves matter most when you genuinely want to transfer responsibility for a value permanently (e.g. handing off a resource into a struct that will own it going forward).

Next: [Move Semantics](/rust/move-semantics/).

Ownership and Memory.Move Semantics:
## Why moves happen

When you write `let s2 = s1;` for a heap-allocated type like `String`, Rust does **not** deep-copy the heap data (that would be slow, and unnecessary most of the time). It also doesn't just copy the pointer and leave both variables pointing at the same memory (that would violate rule 2 — "only one owner" — and would cause a **double-free** when both `s1` and `s2` went out of scope and both tried to free the same memory).

Instead, Rust does something in between: it copies the small stack part (the pointer, length, and capacity — collectively called the `String`'s "header") to `s2`, and then considers `s1` **invalid**. This is a **move**. No heap data is copied; ownership simply transfers.

```rust
let s1 = String::from("hello");
let s2 = s1; // the String's heap data is NOT copied.
             // s1's (pointer, len, capacity) is copied to s2.
             // s1 is now invalid — using it is a compile error.
```

Visually, before the move, `s1` points to heap memory containing `"hello"`. After `let s2 = s1;`, `s2` points to that same heap memory, and `s1` no longer exists as far as the compiler is concerned — there's no risk of both trying to free it, because only one of them (`s2`) is considered valid.

## Moves happen on function calls and returns too

```rust
fn main() {
    let s1 = gives_ownership();          // return value moves into s1
    let s2 = String::from("hello");
    let s3 = takes_and_gives_back(s2);   // s2 moves in, return value moves into s3

    // s2 is invalid here — it was moved into the function
}

fn gives_ownership() -> String {
    String::from("yours")
}

fn takes_and_gives_back(a_string: String) -> String {
    a_string // returned, ownership moves out to the caller
}
```

## Moves in loops and collections

Iterating over a `Vec<String>` by value moves each element out one at a time:

```rust
let names = vec![String::from("Alice"), String::from("Bob")];

for name in names { // this moves `names` — it's no longer usable after the loop
    println!("{name}");
}

// println!("{:?}", names); // ERROR: names was moved
```

If you need to keep using the collection afterward, iterate by reference instead (`for name in &names`) — this is a preview of borrowing, covered next.

## What actually gets "moved" — and what doesn't

Moves apply to types that own heap data or other resources: `String`, `Vec<T>`, `Box<T>`, and most custom `struct`s and `enum`s you define. Simple stack-only types like `i32`, `f64`, `bool`, and `char` behave differently — assigning them doesn't move, it **copies**, and the original stays valid:

```rust
let x = 5;
let y = x; // this COPIES, not moves — both x and y are valid
println!("{x} {y}"); // fine — no error
```

Why the difference? Because copying a stack-only integer is trivially cheap (a few bytes), Rust just does it automatically rather than making you think about ownership for every single number. This behavior is controlled by a trait called `Copy`, covered in depth on the next page.

Next: [Clone and Copy](/rust/clone-and-copy/).

Ownership and Memory.References and Borrowing:
Moving ownership into every function that needs to look at a value would make Rust nearly unusable — you'd have to return everything back out of every function just to keep using it in the caller. The real solution, and the tool you'll use constantly, is **borrowing**: giving a function temporary access to a value via a **reference**, without transferring ownership.

## Immutable references: `&`

```rust
fn main() {
    let s1 = String::from("hello");
    let len = calculate_length(&s1); // pass a REFERENCE, not s1 itself

    println!("The length of '{s1}' is {len}."); // s1 still valid here!
}

fn calculate_length(s: &String) -> usize {
    s.len()
} // s goes out of scope here, but because it's just a reference (doesn't own
  // the data), nothing is dropped — the original String is untouched
```

`&s1` creates a reference to `s1` — think of it as an arrow pointing at the data, without owning it. The function signature `fn calculate_length(s: &String)` declares that it borrows a `String` rather than taking ownership of one. Because ownership never transferred, `s1` is completely unaffected and still usable in `main` after the call. This is called **borrowing**.

## Mutable references: `&mut`

By default, a reference is read-only — you can't modify data through it. If you need to modify borrowed data, use `&mut`:

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut s = String::from("hello");
    change(&mut s);
    println!("{s}"); // "hello, world"
}

fn change(s: &mut String) {
    s.push_str(", world");
}
```

Note that `s` itself must be declared `mut` in `main` for this to work — you can't create a mutable reference to an immutable variable.

## The borrowing rules

This is the part that the compiler enforces strictly, and it's worth learning precisely because it's the source of most "fighting the borrow checker" moments as a beginner. At any given time, for a particular piece of data, you can have **either**:

- **any number of immutable references** (`&T`), **or**
- **exactly one mutable reference** (`&mut T`)

...but never both kinds at the same time.

```rust
let mut s = String::from("hello");

let r1 = &s; // ok
let r2 = &s; // ok, multiple immutable references are fine
println!("{r1} {r2}");

let r3 = &mut s; // ERROR if r1/r2 are still in use: cannot borrow as mutable
                  // because it's also borrowed as immutable
println!("{r3}");
```

Why does this rule exist? It's the compile-time version of preventing **data races**: if one part of your code could read data while another part simultaneously modifies it, you get unpredictable behavior. By ensuring at compile time that a mutable borrow is always exclusive, Rust makes data races on shared data structurally impossible — not "unlikely if you're careful," but genuinely impossible, because the code wouldn't compile otherwise. This same guarantee is what makes Rust's approach to multithreading (covered in [Concurrency](/rust/threads/)) so much safer than in most languages.

## Non-Lexical Lifetimes: references end at last use, not end of scope

Modern Rust is smart about *when* a borrow actually ends — a reference's borrow ends at its **last use**, not necessarily at the end of the enclosing block:

```rust
let mut s = String::from("hello");

let r1 = &s;
let r2 = &s;
println!("{r1} {r2}"); // r1 and r2's last use — borrow effectively ends here

let r3 = &mut s; // fine! r1 and r2 are no longer "in use"
println!("{r3}");
```

This makes the borrow checker considerably more permissive than a naive "scopes can never overlap" rule would suggest, and is why a lot of code that looks like it should conflict actually compiles fine.

Next: [The Borrow Checker Explained](/rust/the-borrow-checker-explained/), which works through common error messages you'll actually see and how to read them.

Ownership and Memory.The Borrow Checker Explained:
The **borrow checker** is the part of the Rust compiler that enforces the ownership and borrowing rules from the previous pages. This page is a practical guide to the errors it produces and how to fix them — because as a beginner, you *will* see these, and the goal is to recognize them instantly rather than be confused by them.

## "cannot move out of borrowed content" / "value moved here"

```rust
fn main() {
    let s1 = String::from("hello");
    let s2 = s1;

    println!("{s1}"); // error: value borrowed here after move
}
```

**Fix**: decide what you actually want. If you need both `s1` and `s2` as independent values, clone: `let s2 = s1.clone();`. If you only needed a temporary look at the value, borrow instead: use `&s1` wherever you were going to use `s1` after the "move."

## "cannot borrow as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable"

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let first = &v[0];
    v.push(4); // error
    println!("{first}");
}
```

**Why**: `first` borrows from `v`. `v.push(4)` might reallocate `v`'s internal buffer to make room for the new element, which would leave `first` pointing at freed memory — a classic use-after-free bug in C. The borrow checker catches this at compile time instead of letting it become a runtime crash or, worse, silent memory corruption.

**Fix**: reorder so the immutable borrow's last use happens before the mutable borrow starts:

```rust
let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
v.push(4);
let first = &v[0]; // borrow AFTER the mutation
println!("{first}");
```

## "cannot borrow `x` as mutable more than once at a time"

```rust
let mut s = String::from("hello");
let r1 = &mut s;
let r2 = &mut s; // error
println!("{r1} {r2}");
```

**Fix**: you almost never actually need two simultaneous mutable references to the same data. Restructure so one mutable borrow's work finishes (its last use) before the next one begins, or reconsider whether you need one function that does both operations instead of two separate borrows.

## Dangling references — the bug this whole system prevents

```rust
fn dangle() -> &String { // error: missing lifetime, and conceptually broken
    let s = String::from("hello");
    &s
} // s goes out of scope and is dropped HERE — so the reference we tried
  // to return would point at freed memory
```

The compiler rejects this outright. In C or C++, this exact pattern compiles fine and produces a **dangling pointer** — a reference to memory that's already been freed, leading to undefined behavior the first time it's used. This is genuinely one of the most common and dangerous classes of bugs in unsafe languages, and Rust's borrow checker eliminates it entirely, at compile time, in every single case, rather than relying on the developer to catch it in testing.

**Fix**: return the owned value instead of a reference to a local:

```rust
fn no_dangle() -> String {
    let s = String::from("hello");
    s // ownership moves out to the caller — no dangling reference
}
```

## The mindset shift

Beginners often describe this process as "fighting the borrow checker." With practice, that feeling fades, because the rules map onto a mental model that becomes second nature: *"who owns this, and is anyone else looking at it right now?"* Every error above is the compiler catching a real bug that would otherwise exist silently in your program — the borrow checker isn't being difficult for its own sake, it's finding actual memory bugs before your program ever runs.

