Ownership and Memory.Slices:
A **slice** lets you reference a contiguous sequence of elements within a collection, rather than the whole thing, without taking ownership.

## String slices

```rust
fn main() {
    let s = String::from("hello world");

    let hello = &s[0..5];  // "hello"
    let world = &s[6..11]; // "world"

    println!("{hello} {world}");
}
```

`&s[0..5]` is a `&str` — a reference to a portion of `s`'s data, specified as a byte range. Shorthand syntax: `&s[..5]` (from the start), `&s[6..]` (to the end), `&s[..]` (the whole string as a slice).

A very common and useful pattern is writing functions that accept `&str` instead of `&String`, because a `&String` can always be used where a `&str` is expected (via automatic deref coercion), but not the reverse — so `&str` parameters are more flexible:

```rust
fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {
    let bytes = s.as_bytes();

    for (i, &item) in bytes.iter().enumerate() {
        if item == b' ' {
            return &s[..i];
        }
    }

    &s[..]
}

fn main() {
    let my_string = String::from("hello world");
    let word = first_word(&my_string); // works: &String coerces to &str
    println!("{word}"); // "hello"

    let literal = "hello world";
    let word2 = first_word(literal); // also works directly — literal is already &str
    println!("{word2}");
}
```

**Important**: string slicing works on *byte* indices, and Rust strings are UTF-8. Slicing at a byte index that isn't on a character boundary (e.g. in the middle of a multi-byte emoji) causes a panic at runtime. This is covered further in [Working With Text](/rust/working-with-text/).

## Array and vector slices

The same idea applies to any sequence, using `&[T]`:

```rust
fn main() {
    let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    let slice: &[i32] = &a[1..3]; // [2, 3]

    println!("{:?}", slice);

    let v = vec![10, 20, 30, 40];
    let v_slice = &v[..2]; // [10, 20]
    println!("{:?}", v_slice);
}
```

Just like `&str` is the "borrow anything string-like" type, `&[T]` is the "borrow anything sequence-like" type — writing functions that take `&[T]` instead of `&Vec<T>` lets them accept arrays, vectors, or other slices uniformly:

```rust
fn sum(numbers: &[i32]) -> i32 {
    numbers.iter().sum()
}

fn main() {
    let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let a = [4, 5, 6];

    println!("{}", sum(&v));  // works — &Vec<i32> coerces to &[i32]
    println!("{}", sum(&a));  // works — &[i32; 3] coerces to &[i32]
}
```

Next: [Lifetimes Introduction](/rust/lifetimes-introduction/).

Ownership and Memory.Lifetimes Introduction:
Lifetimes are Rust's way of making sure references never outlive the data they point to — a compile-time-only concept with **zero runtime cost**. This page is a gentle introduction; you'll get plenty of practice reading (and occasionally writing) lifetime annotations as you work through real code, but as a beginner you won't need to write them explicitly nearly as often as this page might suggest, because Rust infers them in the vast majority of everyday cases.

## The problem lifetimes solve

```rust
fn longest(x: &str, y: &str) -> &str { // ERROR: missing lifetime specifier
    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
}
```

This function takes two string references and returns one of them — but *which one* depends on runtime values (their lengths), and the compiler can't know in advance which one will be returned. Without more information, it can't verify that the returned reference will still be valid at the call site, because it doesn't know how long `x` and `y` themselves are guaranteed to live. So it asks you to be explicit.

## Lifetime annotations

```rust
fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
}
```

`'a` (read "tick-a" or "lifetime a") is a generic lifetime parameter, declared in angle brackets like a generic type. This signature says: *"the returned reference will be valid for exactly as long as the shorter of `x`'s and `y`'s lifetimes."* Crucially, **lifetime annotations don't change how long anything actually lives** — they don't extend or shorten any value's real lifetime. They're purely a description, for the compiler's benefit, of a relationship that already exists in your code, so it can verify the function is being used safely at every call site.

```rust
fn main() {
    let string1 = String::from("long string is long");
    let result;
    {
        let string2 = String::from("xyz");
        result = longest(string1.as_str(), string2.as_str());
        println!("Longest: {result}"); // fine — used while string2 is still alive
    }
    // println!("{result}"); // ERROR if uncommented: string2 doesn't live long enough
}
```

The compiler uses the `'a` annotation to figure out that `result`'s validity is tied to the shorter-lived of the two inputs (`string2`), and correctly rejects using `result` after `string2` is dropped.

## Lifetime elision: why you rarely write these

Rust has a set of rules (called **lifetime elision rules**) that let the compiler infer lifetimes automatically in common patterns, so most functions never need explicit annotations:

```rust
fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str { // no lifetime annotations needed here —
    // ...                       // the compiler infers the output borrows from `s`
}
```

You only need explicit lifetime annotations when a function has **multiple** reference parameters and the compiler can't determine on its own which one the output is tied to — exactly the `longest` situation above. In everyday application code, you'll write far more code *without* explicit lifetimes than with them; struct definitions that hold references are the other common place you'll see `'a`, but many beginner and intermediate programs avoid storing references in structs at all (favoring owned `String`/`Vec` fields instead), which sidesteps the need for lifetime annotations almost entirely until you're more comfortable.

## The 'static lifetime

One special lifetime you will see often: `'static`, meaning "valid for the entire duration of the program." String literals are always `'static`, because their text is baked directly into the compiled binary:

```rust
let s: &'static str = "I live for the whole program";
```

This wraps up ownership and memory — the conceptual core of Rust. Everything from here on (structs, enums, error handling, collections) builds on these ideas, but mostly just *uses* them rather than introducing new rules. Next: [If and Else](/rust/if-and-else/).

Control Flow and Functions.If and Else:
```rust
fn main() {
    let number = 7;

    if number < 5 {
        println!("less than five");
    } else if number == 5 {
        println!("exactly five");
    } else {
        println!("greater than five");
    }
}
```

No parentheses are required around the condition (unlike C or Java), but the braces are mandatory even for single-line bodies — Rust has no "if without braces" shorthand, which avoids an entire class of dangling-else bugs.

**The condition must be an actual `bool`.** There's no automatic truthiness for numbers, strings, or `Option`s like in Python or JavaScript:

```rust
let number = 3;
if number { // ERROR: expected bool, found integer
    // ...
}
```

You must write an explicit comparison: `if number != 0 { ... }`.

## if as an expression

As covered in [Expressions vs Statements](/rust/expressions-vs-statements/), `if` produces a value, so it's common to use it directly in a `let`:

```rust
let condition = true;
let number = if condition { 5 } else { 6 };
```

Both branches must evaluate to the **same type** — you can't do `if condition { 5 } else { "six" }`, since the compiler needs to know `number`'s type at compile time and an integer/string mismatch can't be resolved.

Next: [Loops: loop, while, for](/rust/loops-loop-while-for/).

Control Flow and Functions.Loops: loop, while, for:
Rust has three loop constructs, each suited to a different situation.

## loop: infinite, until you break

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut count = 0;
    loop {
        count += 1;
        if count == 5 {
            break;
        }
    }
    println!("{count}"); // 5
}
```

`loop` runs forever until an explicit `break`. Unlike the other two loop types, `loop` is itself an **expression** — `break` can carry a value out of it:

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut counter = 0;

    let result = loop {
        counter += 1;
        if counter == 10 {
            break counter * 2; // this value becomes the loop's value
        }
    };

    println!("{result}"); // 20
}
```

This is genuinely useful for retry loops, or any "keep trying until this condition, then produce a result" pattern.

## Loop labels

For nested loops, you can label loops and `break`/`continue` a specific one:

```rust
'outer: for x in 0..5 {
    for y in 0..5 {
        if y == 2 {
            continue 'outer;
        }
        if x == 3 {
            break 'outer;
        }
        println!("{x},{y}");
    }
}
```

## while: conditional loop

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut number = 3;

    while number != 0 {
        println!("{number}!");
        number -= 1;
    }

    println!("LIFTOFF!");
}
```

Same idea as `while` in most other languages: check the condition, run the body if true, repeat.

## for: iterating over a collection (the most common loop in Rust)

```rust
fn main() {
    let v = vec![10, 20, 30];

    for element in &v {
        println!("{element}");
    }

    for i in 0..5 {       // exclusive range: 0,1,2,3,4
        println!("{i}");
    }

    for i in (0..5).rev() { // reversed: 4,3,2,1,0
        println!("{i}");
    }
}
```

`for element in &v` borrows each element by reference — the collection remains usable afterward (see [Ownership Rules](/rust/ownership-rules/) for why this matters). `for` is idiomatic Rust's default choice for iterating over anything — a `while` loop with a manual index and bounds check, common in C, is both more verbose and more error-prone (off-by-one bugs, out-of-bounds panics) than iterating directly with `for`.

## Which loop to reach for

- **`for`** — iterating over a known collection or range. Use this by default; it's the most common loop by far in real Rust code.
- **`while`** — looping based on a condition whose end isn't a simple count (waiting for external state to change, processing until a sentinel value).
- **`loop`** — when you need an explicit `break` with a value, or a genuinely unconditional retry/poll loop.

Next: [Match Expressions](/rust/match-expressions/).

Control Flow and Functions.Match Expressions:
`match` is Rust's pattern-matching control flow construct — more powerful than a `switch` statement in C-family languages, and used constantly in idiomatic Rust code, especially with enums (covered fully in [Pattern Matching Deep Dive](/rust/pattern-matching-deep-dive/)). This page introduces the basics.

```rust
fn main() {
    let number = 3;

    match number {
        1 => println!("one"),
        2 => println!("two"),
        3 => println!("three"),
        _ => println!("something else"), // catch-all, REQUIRED if not all cases covered
    }
}
```

## match is exhaustive — this is enforced by the compiler

Unlike `switch` in C, Java, or JavaScript, Rust's `match` **must** cover every possible value, or the code won't compile. The `_` pattern is a catch-all that matches anything not covered above it. This exhaustiveness check is genuinely valuable: if you later add a new variant to an enum you're matching on elsewhere in a large codebase, the compiler will point out every single `match` that now needs updating, rather than silently falling through and causing a bug.

## Matching multiple patterns and ranges

```rust
let x = 5;

match x {
    1 | 2 => println!("one or two"),
    3..=5 => println!("three through five"),
    _ => println!("anything else"),
}
```

## match as an expression

Like `if`, `match` produces a value:

```rust
let x = 5;
let description = match x {
    1 => "one",
    2 => "two",
    _ => "other",
};
println!("{description}");
```

## Matching and binding values

This is where `match` becomes much more powerful than a simple `switch` — patterns can extract data out of the value being matched:

```rust
enum Shape {
    Circle(f64),           // radius
    Rectangle(f64, f64),   // width, height
}

fn area(shape: &Shape) -> f64 {
    match shape {
        Shape::Circle(radius) => std::f64::consts::PI * radius * radius,
        Shape::Rectangle(width, height) => width * height,
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Shape::Circle(2.0);
    let r = Shape::Rectangle(3.0, 4.0);
    println!("{}", area(&c)); // 12.566...
    println!("{}", area(&r)); // 12
}
```

Each arm doesn't just check *which variant* it is — it simultaneously pulls out the data inside that variant (`radius`, or `width` and `height`) and binds it to a name you can use in that arm's code. This combination — matching a shape *and* extracting its data in one step — is used everywhere in idiomatic Rust, especially with `Option` and `Result` (covered in [Option and Null Safety](/rust/option-and-null-safety/) and [Result and Recoverable Errors](/rust/result-and-recoverable-errors/)).

Next: [Functions](/rust/functions/).

Control Flow and Functions.Functions:
```rust
fn main() {
    greet("Ferris");
    let sum = add(2, 3);
    println!("{sum}");
}

fn greet(name: &str) {
    println!("Hello, {name}!");
}

fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b // no semicolon = return value (see Expressions vs Statements)
}
```

## Key rules

- **Parameter types are always required** — Rust never infers a function's parameter types from how it's called, unlike some languages. `fn add(a: i32, b: i32)` — the `: i32` is mandatory.
- **Return type** is declared after `->`. If a function returns nothing meaningful, you omit `-> Type` entirely (it implicitly returns `()`, the unit type).
- Functions can be defined **in any order**, and even after they're used, as long as they're in scope — unlike some languages, Rust doesn't require forward declarations.
- **snake_case** is the naming convention: `calculate_total`, not `calculateTotal` or `CalculateTotal`.

## Multiple return values via tuples

```rust
fn min_max(numbers: &[i32]) -> (i32, i32) {
    let mut min = numbers[0];
    let mut max = numbers[0];
    for &n in numbers {
        if n < min { min = n; }
        if n > max { max = n; }
    }
    (min, max)
}

fn main() {
    let (smallest, largest) = min_max(&[4, 1, 9, 2]);
    println!("{smallest} {largest}"); // 1 9
}
```

## Explicit return

```rust
fn classify(n: i32) -> &'static str {
    if n < 0 {
        return "negative"; // early return needs the keyword
    }
    if n == 0 {
        return "zero";
    }
    "positive" // final expression, implicit return
}
```

Both styles are idiomatic — use explicit `return` for early exits, and the final-expression style for the "normal" return path.

## Never-returning functions

Occasionally a function never returns at all (it always panics, loops forever, or exits the process). This is expressed with the `!` "never" type:

```rust
fn forever() -> ! {
    loop {
        // ...
    }
}
```

You won't write this often as a beginner, but you'll see it in signatures like `std::process::exit`'s, and it's useful to recognize.

Next: [Closures](/rust/closures/).

Control Flow and Functions.Closures:
A **closure** is an anonymous function you can store in a variable, pass as an argument, or return from another function — and, unlike a regular `fn`, a closure can *capture* variables from the scope it was defined in.

```rust
fn main() {
    let add_one = |x: i32| x + 1;
    println!("{}", add_one(5)); // 6

    let multiply = |x: i32, y: i32| -> i32 { x * y };
    println!("{}", multiply(3, 4)); // 12
}
```

Syntax: parameters between `|pipes|`, then the body (a single expression, or a `{ }` block for multiple statements). Type annotations on parameters and return type are usually **optional** — the compiler infers them from how the closure is first used, unlike a top-level `fn` where they're always required.

## Capturing the environment

This is the defining feature that separates closures from regular functions:

```rust
fn main() {
    let x = 10;
    let add_x = |n| n + x; // captures `x` from the surrounding scope

    println!("{}", add_x(5)); // 15
}
```

A plain `fn` cannot do this — `fn add_x(n: i32) -> i32 { n + x }` at the top level wouldn't compile, because top-level functions have no access to outer local variables at all. Closures can, and this is exactly what makes them useful for passing custom, context-aware behavior into another function.

## Closures as function arguments — the most common use case

Closures show up constantly as arguments to iterator methods (covered fully in [Iterators](/rust/iterators/)):

```rust
fn main() {
    let numbers = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

    let doubled: Vec<i32> = numbers.iter().map(|n| n * 2).collect();
    println!("{doubled:?}"); // [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]

    let evens: Vec<&i32> = numbers.iter().filter(|&&n| n % 2 == 0).collect();
    println!("{evens:?}"); // [2, 4]
}
```

`map` and `filter` each take a closure describing exactly what to do with each element — this pattern (passing behavior as a value) is one of the most common things you'll do in everyday Rust code.

## How closures capture: by reference, by mutable reference, or by move

By default, a closure borrows captured variables (immutably, or mutably if it modifies them) — just enough access to do its job. If you need the closure to *own* the captured data (common when the closure needs to outlive the current scope — e.g. moving into a new thread), use the `move` keyword:

```rust
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let data = vec![1, 2, 3];

    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
        println!("{data:?}"); // the closure now OWNS `data`
    });

    handle.join().unwrap();
}
```

Without `move`, the closure would try to borrow `data`, but the compiler can't guarantee the borrow stays valid for as long as the spawned thread might run — so `thread::spawn` effectively requires `move` closures for anything they capture. This pattern is covered fully in [Threads](/rust/threads/).

Next: [Function Pointers](/rust/function-pointers/).

Control Flow and Functions.Function Pointers:
Closures aren't the only way to pass "behavior" around as a value — plain functions can be passed by their name too, using the `fn` type (a **function pointer** type, distinct from the `Fn`/`FnMut`/`FnOnce` traits closures implement).

```rust
fn add_one(x: i32) -> i32 {
    x + 1
}

fn apply(f: fn(i32) -> i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    f(value)
}

fn main() {
    let result = apply(add_one, 5);
    println!("{result}"); // 6
}
```

`fn(i32) -> i32` is a type describing "a function taking an `i32`, returning an `i32`" — `add_one` fits that shape, so it can be passed directly where that type is expected.

## fn vs closures: which type to accept as a parameter

In practice, when *writing* a function that takes "some callable" as a parameter, you'll almost always want to accept a closure trait bound (`Fn`, `FnMut`, or `FnOnce` — covered in [Traits and Trait Bounds](/rust/traits-and-trait-bounds/)) rather than the narrower `fn` pointer type, because a generic closure parameter accepts plain functions, closures with no captures, *and* closures that do capture variables — while a plain `fn` parameter only accepts the first two.

```rust
fn apply_twice<F: Fn(i32) -> i32>(f: F, value: i32) -> i32 {
    f(f(value))
}

fn main() {
    let result = apply_twice(|x| x + 1, 5); // works with a closure...
    println!("{result}"); // 7

    let result2 = apply_twice(add_one, 5); // ...and with a plain function
    println!("{result2}"); // 7
}

fn add_one(x: i32) -> i32 {
    x + 1
}
```

As a beginner, the practical takeaway: you'll write closures far more often than you'll write the `fn(...)` pointer type explicitly. Recognizing `fn(i32) -> i32` as a type when you see it in someone else's code (often in lower-level or FFI-adjacent code) is the main goal here.

This wraps up control flow and functions — the everyday tools for writing logic. Next: [Defining Structs](/rust/defining-structs/), where we start building our own data types.

Structs, Enums and Pattern Matching.Defining Structs:
A **struct** groups related data together under one named type — similar to a class's fields in Java/C#, or a dataclass in Python, minus the built-in methods (those come from `impl` blocks, covered next).

## Defining and instantiating

```rust
struct User {
    username: String,
    email: String,
    active: bool,
    sign_in_count: u64,
}

fn main() {
    let user1 = User {
        username: String::from("ferris"),
        email: String::from("ferris@rust-lang.org"),
        active: true,
        sign_in_count: 1,
    };

    println!("{}", user1.username);
}
```

Field order in the instantiation doesn't have to match the definition, but every field must be provided. Access fields with dot notation: `user1.username`.

## Mutability is all-or-nothing

```rust
let mut user1 = User { /* ... */ };
user1.email = String::from("new@example.com"); // fine, user1 is mut

let user2 = User { /* ... */ };
user2.email = String::from("x"); // ERROR: user2 is not mutable
```

You can't mark individual fields as mutable — the whole instance is either mutable or it isn't.

## Field init shorthand

When a variable name matches a field name exactly, you can skip repeating it:

```rust
fn build_user(username: String, email: String) -> User {
    User {
        username, // shorthand for `username: username`
        email,
        active: true,
        sign_in_count: 1,
    }
}
```

## Struct update syntax

Build a new instance based on an existing one, overriding only some fields:

```rust
let user2 = User {
    email: String::from("another@example.com"),
    ..user1 // fill remaining fields from user1
};
```

Note: this *moves* any non-`Copy` fields out of `user1` (like `username`, a `String`) — so `user1` may become partially invalid afterward, following the same move rules from [Move Semantics](/rust/move-semantics/).

## Tuple structs

Structs without named fields, useful for lightweight wrapper types:

```rust
struct Point(i32, i32, i32);
struct Color(u8, u8, u8);

let origin = Point(0, 0, 0);
println!("{}", origin.0); // access by index, like a tuple
```

`Point` and `Color` are distinct types even though both are structurally "three numbers" — this gives you type safety that a bare tuple wouldn't (a function expecting `Color` won't accidentally accept a `Point`).

## Unit-like structs

Structs with no fields at all — useful when you need a type to implement a trait on, but don't need to store any data:

```rust
struct AlwaysEqual;

let subject = AlwaysEqual;
```

Next: [Methods and impl Blocks](/rust/methods-and-impl-blocks/).

Structs, Enums and Pattern Matching.Methods and impl Blocks:
Methods are functions associated with a specific type, defined inside an `impl` (implementation) block.

```rust
struct Rectangle {
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
}

impl Rectangle {
    fn area(&self) -> u32 {
        self.width * self.height
    }

    fn can_hold(&self, other: &Rectangle) -> bool {
        self.width > other.width && self.height > other.height
    }
}

fn main() {
    let rect1 = Rectangle { width: 30, height: 50 };
    let rect2 = Rectangle { width: 10, height: 40 };

    println!("Area: {}", rect1.area());
    println!("Can hold: {}", rect1.can_hold(&rect2));
}
```

## &self, &mut self, self

Every method's first parameter describes how it interacts with the instance:

- **`&self`** — borrows the instance immutably. Most methods use this: they read data but don't need to change or consume it.
- **`&mut self`** — borrows mutably, for methods that modify the instance.
- **`self`** (no `&`) — takes ownership of the instance, consuming it. Used when a method transforms an instance into something else and the original shouldn't be usable afterward.

```rust
impl Rectangle {
    fn set_width(&mut self, width: u32) {
        self.width = width;
    }

    fn into_square(self) -> Rectangle { // consumes self
        let side = self.width.max(self.height);
        Rectangle { width: side, height: side }
    }
}
```

`self` is shorthand for `self: Self`, where `Self` (capitalized) refers to "whatever type this `impl` block is for" — you'll see `Self` used as a return type constantly, especially in constructors (next).

## Associated functions (no self — "static methods")

Functions inside an `impl` block that *don't* take `self` are called with `Type::function_name(...)` rather than `instance.method(...)`. The most common example is a constructor, conventionally named `new`:

```rust
impl Rectangle {
    fn new(width: u32, height: u32) -> Self {
        Self { width, height }
    }

    fn square(size: u32) -> Self {
        Self { width: size, height: size }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let rect = Rectangle::new(30, 50);
    let sq = Rectangle::square(20);
}
```

There's no built-in constructor syntax or keyword in Rust (no `__init__`, no `constructor()`) — `new` is purely a convention, not a special language feature, but it's such a strong convention that virtually every type in the standard library and ecosystem follows it.

## Multiple impl blocks

You can split a type's methods across multiple `impl` blocks (useful for organizing trait implementations separately, covered in [Traits and Trait Bounds](/rust/traits-and-trait-bounds/)) — the compiler treats them as one combined set of methods:

```rust
impl Rectangle {
    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.width * self.height }
}

impl Rectangle {
    fn perimeter(&self) -> u32 { 2 * (self.width + self.height) }
}
```

Next: [Enums](/rust/enums/).

Structs, Enums and Pattern Matching.Enums:
An **enum** (enumeration) defines a type by listing all its possible **variants**. Rust enums are dramatically more powerful than enums in C, Java, or most other languages, because each variant can carry its own associated data — this makes them one of Rust's most-used and most-loved features.

## Basic enums

```rust
enum Direction {
    North,
    South,
    East,
    West,
}

fn main() {
    let heading = Direction::North;
}
```

## Variants with data

This is where Rust enums go beyond a simple list of names — each variant can hold different data, of different shapes:

```rust
enum Message {
    Quit,                       // no data
    Move { x: i32, y: i32 },    // named fields, like a struct
    Write(String),              // one unnamed field, like a tuple
    ChangeColor(i32, i32, i32), // multiple unnamed fields
}

fn main() {
    let messages = vec![
        Message::Quit,
        Message::Move { x: 10, y: 20 },
        Message::Write(String::from("hello")),
        Message::ChangeColor(255, 0, 0),
    ];
}
```

A single `Message` type here represents four genuinely different shapes of data — an alternative in a language without this feature would require four separate structs plus some way to tell them apart at runtime (often an error-prone tag field you manage by hand). Rust's enum handles both the "which kind is this" tagging *and* the associated data in one type, and — critically — `match` (covered next) forces you to handle every variant, so you can never forget a case.

## Methods on enums

Just like structs, enums can have an `impl` block:

```rust
impl Message {
    fn describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            Message::Quit => String::from("quit"),
            Message::Move { x, y } => format!("move to ({x}, {y})"),
            Message::Write(text) => format!("write: {text}"),
            Message::ChangeColor(r, g, b) => format!("color: {r},{g},{b}"),
        }
    }
}
```

## The most important enums in Rust: Option and Result

Two enums from the standard library are used so pervasively that they deserve their own pages: `Option<T>` (representing "a value, or nothing" — Rust's alternative to null, covered next in [Option and Null Safety](/rust/option-and-null-safety/)) and `Result<T, E>` (representing "success with a value, or failure with an error," covered in [Result and Recoverable Errors](/rust/result-and-recoverable-errors/)). Understanding that these are *just regular enums*, defined using exactly the syntax on this page, demystifies a lot of Rust code you'll read early on:

```rust
// (from the standard library, simplified)
enum Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None,
}

enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),
    Err(E),
}
```

Next: [Option and Null Safety](/rust/option-and-null-safety/).

Structs, Enums and Pattern Matching.Option and Null Safety:
Rust has **no `null`**. This is a deliberate design decision — the inventor of null himself, Tony Hoare, called it his "billion dollar mistake," because a variable that can silently be null forces every single use of it to either remember to check, or risk crashing at runtime with a null pointer exception. Rust closes this gap entirely at the type system level, using `Option<T>`.

## Option<T>

```rust
enum Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None,
}
```

`Option<T>` is a regular enum (from [Enums](/rust/enums/)) with two variants: `Some(value)` when there's a value present, `None` when there isn't. It's so fundamental that it's included in Rust's "prelude" — automatically available everywhere, no `use` statement needed.

```rust
fn main() {
    let some_number: Option<i32> = Some(5);
    let no_number: Option<i32> = None;

    let some_string = Some("a string");
}
```

## Why this eliminates null pointer bugs

The key insight: `Option<i32>` and `i32` are **different types**. You cannot use an `Option<i32>` in a context expecting a plain `i32` without first handling the `None` case — the compiler simply won't let you:

```rust
let x: i32 = 5;
let y: Option<i32> = Some(5);

let sum = x + y; // ERROR: cannot add `i32` and `Option<i32>`
```

This forces you to explicitly deal with the "maybe nothing" case *before* you can get at the value — you can't accidentally forget to check, the way you can accidentally forget a null check in Java or a `None` check in Python, because the compiler won't even let the mismatched types compile.

## Getting the value out

**match** — the exhaustive, always-safe way:

```rust
fn describe(x: Option<i32>) -> String {
    match x {
        Some(value) => format!("got {value}"),
        None => String::from("nothing here"),
    }
}
```

**if let** — concise, for when you only care about one case (full coverage in [if let and while let](/rust/if-let-and-while-let/)):

```rust
let config_value: Option<&str> = Some("production");

if let Some(value) = config_value {
    println!("using {value}");
} else {
    println!("using default");
}
```

**Common helper methods**:

```rust
let x: Option<i32> = Some(5);

x.is_some();               // true
x.is_none();                // false
x.unwrap();                  // 5 — PANICS if x is None, use with caution
x.unwrap_or(0);               // 5, or 0 if x were None — safe default
x.unwrap_or_else(|| compute()); // like unwrap_or, but the default is computed lazily
x.map(|n| n * 2);              // Some(10) — transforms the inner value if present
x.expect("x should have a value"); // like unwrap, but with a custom panic message
```

`.unwrap()` is common in quick scripts and examples (including plenty in this guide), but in real production code, prefer `match`, `if let`, `unwrap_or`, or the `?` operator (covered in [The Question Mark Operator](/rust/the-question-mark-operator/)) — reaching for `.unwrap()` reintroduces the exact "might crash at this line" risk that `Option` exists to prevent, just moved from "might be null" to "might panic here."

Next: [Pattern Matching Deep Dive](/rust/pattern-matching-deep-dive/).

Structs, Enums and Pattern Matching.Pattern Matching Deep Dive:
[Match Expressions](/rust/match-expressions/) introduced the basics. This page covers the full range of patterns you can write, which come up constantly once you're comfortable with them.

## Destructuring structs

```rust
struct Point { x: i32, y: i32 }

fn main() {
    let p = Point { x: 0, y: 7 };

    match p {
        Point { x: 0, y: 0 } => println!("origin"),
        Point { x, y: 0 } => println!("on the x-axis at {x}"),
        Point { x: 0, y } => println!("on the y-axis at {y}"),
        Point { x, y } => println!("elsewhere: ({x}, {y})"),
    }
}
```

## Destructuring tuples and nested patterns

```rust
let pair = (1, -1);

match pair {
    (0, y) => println!("first is zero, second is {y}"),
    (x, 0) => println!("first is {x}, second is zero"),
    (x, y) if x == y => println!("equal: {x}"), // "match guard": extra condition
    _ => println!("no special case"),
}
```

That `if x == y` after a pattern is called a **match guard** — an additional boolean condition that must also be true for the arm to match, giving you power beyond what patterns alone can express.

## Ignoring values

```rust
let (x, _, z) = (1, 2, 3); // ignore the middle value entirely
println!("{x} {z}");

fn foo(_: i32, y: i32) -> i32 { // ignore an unused parameter
    y
}

let numbers = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
match numbers {
    (first, .., last) => println!("{first}, {last}"), // .. skips everything between
}
```

`_` ignores a single value; `..` skips any number of remaining values in a tuple, struct, or slice pattern.

## @ bindings — test AND bind in one pattern

```rust
let msg_id = 5;

match msg_id {
    id @ 3..=7 => println!("in range, id = {id}"),
    _ => println!("out of range"),
}
```

`id @ 3..=7` both checks that the value is in the range *and* binds it to `id` for use in the arm — without `@`, you could check the range or bind the value, but not both in one pattern.

## Matching references

When matching against a reference, you often want to match the referenced value directly rather than the reference itself:

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];

for &n in v.iter() { // &n destructures the &i32 reference into a plain i32
    println!("{n}");
}
```

## Refutable vs irrefutable patterns

A pattern is **irrefutable** if it always matches (like `let x = 5;` — `x` matches any value). It's **refutable** if it might not match (like `Some(x)`, which fails to match `None`). `let` requires an irrefutable pattern; `if let` and `match` arms accept refutable ones. This is *why* `let Some(x) = maybe_value;` alone doesn't compile — the compiler won't let you write a `let` that could silently fail to bind anything. You need `if let` (or a full `match`) for that.

Next: [if let and while let](/rust/if-let-and-while-let/).

Structs, Enums and Pattern Matching.if let and while let:
`match` is exhaustive by design — great for correctness, but sometimes verbose when you only care about *one* pattern and want to ignore everything else. `if let` and `while let` are more concise shortcuts for exactly that situation.

## if let

```rust
let config_max: Option<u8> = Some(3);

// The verbose way, with match:
match config_max {
    Some(max) => println!("max is {max}"),
    _ => (),
}

// The concise way, with if let — equivalent for this case:
if let Some(max) = config_max {
    println!("max is {max}");
}
```

`if let Some(max) = config_max` reads as: "if `config_max` matches the pattern `Some(max)`, bind the inner value to `max` and run this block." Unlike `match`, there's no exhaustiveness requirement — you're explicitly opting out of handling every case, which is exactly the point when you only care about one.

## if let / else

```rust
let config_max: Option<u8> = None;

if let Some(max) = config_max {
    println!("max is {max}");
} else {
    println!("no max configured");
}
```

## let else — handling the "other" case by exiting early

A related, newer pattern: `let else` binds a value from a refutable pattern, and requires the `else` branch to diverge (return, break, continue, or panic) if the pattern doesn't match — useful for "bail out early if this isn't what I expect" logic without nesting the rest of the function inside an `if let` block:

```rust
fn describe(config_max: Option<u8>) -> String {
    let Some(max) = config_max else {
        return String::from("no max configured");
    };

    format!("max is {max}") // max is usable here, un-nested
}
```

## while let

Runs a loop for as long as a pattern keeps matching — extremely common for draining a collection or processing values until a sentinel appears:

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut stack = vec![1, 2, 3];

    while let Some(top) = stack.pop() {
        println!("{top}");
    }
    // prints 3, 2, 1 — loop exits automatically once pop() returns None
}
```

`stack.pop()` removes and returns the last element as `Option<T>` (`Some(value)` if the vec wasn't empty, `None` once it is) — `while let` keeps looping exactly as long as it keeps getting `Some`, and stops the instant it gets `None`. This is a clean, idiomatic replacement for the manual "check if empty, then loop, then check again" pattern you'd write in languages without this construct.

This wraps up structs, enums, and pattern matching — the core tools for modeling data in Rust. Next: [Panic and Unrecoverable Errors](/rust/panic-and-unrecoverable-errors/).

Error Handling.Result and Recoverable Errors:
Most errors in real programs are **expected** and **recoverable**: a file might not exist, a network request might time out, user input might be malformed. For these, Rust uses `Result<T, E>` — a regular enum, exactly like `Option<T>`, but carrying error information instead of just "nothing."

```rust
enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),
    Err(E),
}
```

## A basic example

```rust
use std::fs::File;

fn main() {
    let result = File::open("hello.txt");

    let file = match result {
        Ok(file) => file,
        Err(error) => {
            panic!("Problem opening the file: {error:?}");
        }
    };
}
```

`File::open` returns `Result<File, std::io::Error>` — either the opened file, or an error describing what went wrong (file not found, permission denied, etc.). The caller is *forced* to acknowledge both possibilities, because you can't get a `File` out of a `Result<File, Error>` without going through some form of pattern match — the compiler won't let you skip it.

## Matching on different error kinds

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::ErrorKind;

fn main() {
    let result = File::open("hello.txt");

    let file = match result {
        Ok(file) => file,
        Err(error) => match error.kind() {
            ErrorKind::NotFound => {
                File::create("hello.txt").unwrap_or_else(|e| {
                    panic!("Problem creating the file: {e:?}");
                })
            }
            other_error => {
                panic!("Problem opening the file: {other_error:?}");
            }
        },
    };
}
```

## Common Result methods

```rust
let result: Result<i32, String> = Ok(5);

result.is_ok();                 // true
result.is_err();                // false
result.unwrap();                 // 5 — PANICS if it's Err
result.unwrap_or(0);              // 5, or 0 if it were Err
result.unwrap_or_else(|e| 0);      // like unwrap_or, computed lazily from the error
result.expect("should have a value"); // like unwrap, custom panic message
result.map(|n| n * 2);              // Ok(10) — transforms the Ok value if present
result.map_err(|e| format!("wrapped: {e}")); // transforms the Err value if present
```

## Returning Result from your own functions

This is the idiomatic pattern for any function that can fail:

```rust
fn divide(a: f64, b: f64) -> Result<f64, String> {
    if b == 0.0 {
        Err(String::from("cannot divide by zero"))
    } else {
        Ok(a / b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    match divide(10.0, 2.0) {
        Ok(result) => println!("Result: {result}"),
        Err(e) => println!("Error: {e}"),
    }
}
```

Writing functions that return `Result<T, E>` instead of panicking pushes the *decision* of how to handle failure up to the caller, who has more context — a library function generally shouldn't decide to crash your whole program; it should report the problem and let you decide.

Next: [The Question Mark Operator](/rust/the-question-mark-operator/).

Error Handling.The Question Mark Operator:
Chaining several operations that each return `Result` gets verbose fast with explicit `match` at every step. The `?` operator is syntactic sugar that eliminates almost all of that boilerplate.

## The problem it solves

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn read_username_from_file() -> Result<String, io::Error> {
    let username_file_result = File::open("hello.txt");

    let mut username_file = match username_file_result {
        Ok(file) => file,
        Err(e) => return Err(e),
    };

    let mut username = String::new();

    match username_file.read_to_string(&mut username) {
        Ok(_) => Ok(username),
        Err(e) => Err(e),
    }
}
```

That's a lot of repetitive "if it's an error, return it immediately; otherwise, unwrap and keep going" boilerplate for two operations. Here's the same function with `?`:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn read_username_from_file() -> Result<String, io::Error> {
    let mut username_file = File::open("hello.txt")?;
    let mut username = String::new();
    username_file.read_to_string(&mut username)?;
    Ok(username)
}
```

`expr?` means: *"if `expr` is `Ok(value)`, evaluate to `value` and keep going; if it's `Err(e)`, return `Err(e)` from the whole function immediately."* It's exactly the match-and-early-return pattern from above, condensed into one character.

## Chaining with method calls — even shorter

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn read_username_from_file() -> Result<String, io::Error> {
    let mut username = String::new();
    File::open("hello.txt")?.read_to_string(&mut username)?;
    Ok(username)
}
```

Or, since this exact pattern (read an entire file into a String) is so common, the standard library provides a shortcut:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::io;

fn read_username_from_file() -> Result<String, io::Error> {
    fs::read_to_string("hello.txt")
}
```

## ? requires the function to return a compatible type

`?` can only be used inside a function whose return type is `Result<_, E>` (or `Option<_>` — `?` works with `Option` too, returning `None` early on `None`) — the error types don't have to be identical, but the `?` operator needs a way to convert the error it encountered into the function's declared error type, using the `From` trait. This is exactly why custom error types (next page) commonly implement `From` for the various error types they might encounter — it lets `?` convert automatically.

```rust
fn main() -> Result<(), std::io::Error> { // even main can return Result!
    let contents = std::fs::read_to_string("hello.txt")?;
    println!("{contents}");
    Ok(())
}
```

When `main` returns `Result` and the program actually errors out, Rust prints the `Debug` representation of the error and exits with a non-zero code — handy for quick scripts and examples where you don't want to write a full `match` in `main` itself.

Next: [Custom Error Types](/rust/custom-error-types/).

Error Handling.Custom Error Types:
As a program grows, you'll often want a single error type that can represent *any* of the several kinds of failure a function might encounter — parsing errors, I/O errors, validation errors — rather than propagating a generic `String` or a specific library's error type everywhere.

## A manual custom error enum

```rust
use std::fmt;

#[derive(Debug)]
enum AppError {
    NotFound(String),
    InvalidInput { field: String, reason: String },
    IoError(std::io::Error),
}

impl fmt::Display for AppError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            AppError::NotFound(item) => write!(f, "not found: {item}"),
            AppError::InvalidInput { field, reason } => {
                write!(f, "invalid {field}: {reason}")
            }
            AppError::IoError(e) => write!(f, "I/O error: {e}"),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for AppError {}
```

Implementing `std::error::Error` (an empty trait in this case, since `Display` and `Debug` already provide what's needed) marks `AppError` as a "real" error type that's compatible with the wider ecosystem's error-handling conventions — many libraries and functions expect anything they accept as an error to implement this trait.

## Converting between error types with From

To make `?` work seamlessly when a function using `AppError` calls something that returns `std::io::Error`, implement `From`:

```rust
impl From<std::io::Error> for AppError {
    fn from(err: std::io::Error) -> Self {
        AppError::IoError(err)
    }
}

fn read_config() -> Result<String, AppError> {
    let contents = std::fs::read_to_string("config.txt")?; // io::Error auto-converts to AppError!
    Ok(contents)
}
```

Because `AppError` implements `From<std::io::Error>`, the `?` operator on the line above automatically calls `.into()` on the `io::Error` to convert it into an `AppError::IoError` — no manual `.map_err(...)` needed.

## Writing this by hand gets repetitive — that's what thiserror is for

The pattern above (a `Display` impl, an `Error` impl, several `From` impls) is extremely common and extremely repetitive across projects. In practice, almost everyone uses the `thiserror` crate to generate it:

```rust
use thiserror::Error;

#[derive(Error, Debug)]
enum AppError {
    #[error("not found: {0}")]
    NotFound(String),

    #[error("invalid {field}: {reason}")]
    InvalidInput { field: String, reason: String },

    #[error("I/O error: {0}")]
    IoError(#[from] std::io::Error), // #[from] auto-generates the From impl
}
```

This produces exactly the same functionality as the hand-written version above, in far fewer lines. `thiserror` (and its companion `anyhow`, for application-level error handling where you don't need a specific typed error) are covered fully in [anyhow and thiserror](/rust/anyhow-and-thiserror/).

Next: [Error Handling Crates](/rust/error-handling-crates/).

Modules and Crates.The Module Tree and Paths:
Within a single crate, `mod` blocks let you organize code into a tree, similar in spirit to folders and files, but explicit in the source code (or split across files — covered next page).

## Defining modules inline

```rust
mod front_of_house {
    mod hosting {
        fn add_to_waitlist() {}
    }

    mod serving {
        fn take_order() {}
        fn serve_order() {}
    }
}
```

This creates a tree: `front_of_house` contains `hosting` and `serving`, each containing functions. Every item defined this way lives at a specific **path** in that tree, similar to a filesystem path.

## Referring to items with paths

```rust
mod front_of_house {
    pub mod hosting {
        pub fn add_to_waitlist() {}
    }
}

pub fn eat_at_restaurant() {
    // absolute path, starting from the crate root
    crate::front_of_house::hosting::add_to_waitlist();

    // relative path, starting from the current module
    front_of_house::hosting::add_to_waitlist();
}
```

Both absolute (`crate::...`) and relative paths work; which to prefer is largely a style choice — relative paths are common when you're calling something defined nearby, absolute paths when the exact location might move around independently of the calling code.

## use: bringing paths into scope

Typing the full path every time gets tedious — `use` brings a path into scope so you can refer to it by its last segment:

```rust
use crate::front_of_house::hosting;

pub fn eat_at_restaurant() {
    hosting::add_to_waitlist(); // no need for the full path anymore
}
```

Convention: `use` a module's path for functions (`hosting::add_to_waitlist()`, keeping the module name for clarity about where it's from), but `use` the *full* path for structs, enums, and other types (`use std::collections::HashMap;`, then just `HashMap` directly) — this is purely convention, not enforced by the compiler, but it's what you'll see in essentially all real Rust code.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn main() {
    let mut map = HashMap::new(); // not std::collections::HashMap::new()
}
```

## as: renaming on import

```rust
use std::fmt::Result;
use std::io::Result as IoResult; // avoid a name collision

fn function1() -> Result { /* ... */ Ok(()) }
fn function2() -> IoResult<()> { /* ... */ Ok(()) }
```

## Re-exporting with pub use

```rust
mod front_of_house {
    pub mod hosting {
        pub fn add_to_waitlist() {}
    }
}

pub use crate::front_of_house::hosting; // re-export at the crate root

// external code can now call: my_crate::hosting::add_to_waitlist()
// instead of the longer: my_crate::front_of_house::hosting::add_to_waitlist()
```

`pub use` is how library authors present a clean, flat public API while keeping their internal module structure organized however they like internally.

Next: [Separating Modules Into Files](/rust/separating-modules-into-files/).

Modules and Crates.Visibility and pub:
By default, **everything in Rust is private** to the module it's defined in (and that module's descendants) — a stricter default than most languages, and a deliberate one: it means every public API surface is something you opted into explicitly, rather than something you have to remember to lock down.

## The default: private

```rust
mod front_of_house {
    mod hosting {
        fn add_to_waitlist() {} // private — only visible within `hosting` itself
                                  // and anything hosting can see into
    }
}

pub fn eat_at_restaurant() {
    front_of_house::hosting::add_to_waitlist(); // ERROR: hosting is private,
                                                   // add_to_waitlist is private
}
```

## pub: making things visible

```rust
mod front_of_house {
    pub mod hosting {       // module itself must be pub...
        pub fn add_to_waitlist() {} // ...and so must the item inside it
    }
}

pub fn eat_at_restaurant() {
    front_of_house::hosting::add_to_waitlist(); // fine now
}
```

Both the module *and* the item inside it need `pub` — marking only one or the other still leaves the path inaccessible from outside.

## The privacy rule for parent/child modules

A **child module can always see into its parent** (and any ancestor), even without `pub` — privacy in Rust is about hiding implementation details *from the outside*, not about hiding things from your own code:

```rust
fn deliver_order() {} // private function in the crate root

mod back_of_house {
    fn fix_incorrect_order() {
        cook_order();
        crate::deliver_order(); // fine! child modules can always see ancestors
    }

    fn cook_order() {}
}
```

## Struct field visibility

For structs specifically, `pub` on the struct itself doesn't automatically make its fields public — each field needs its own `pub`:

```rust
mod back_of_house {
    pub struct Breakfast {
        pub toast: String,      // public field
        seasonal_fruit: String, // private field — can't be set/read from outside
    }

    impl Breakfast {
        pub fn summer(toast: &str) -> Breakfast {
            Breakfast {
                toast: String::from(toast),
                seasonal_fruit: String::from("peaches"),
            }
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut meal = back_of_house::Breakfast::summer("Rye");
    meal.toast = String::from("Wheat"); // fine, toast is pub
    // meal.seasonal_fruit = String::from("blueberries"); // ERROR: private field
}
```

This pattern — a struct with some public fields and some private ones, plus a public constructor function — is how you enforce invariants: the private `seasonal_fruit` field can only ever be set the way `summer()` sets it, so external code can't put the struct into an invalid state.

## pub(crate): visible within the crate, but not outside it

```rust
pub(crate) fn internal_helper() {} // public within this crate, private to external crates
```

Useful for a library that needs a function accessible across its own internal modules, but doesn't want to commit to it as part of the crate's public API for downstream users.

Next: [Workspaces](/rust/workspaces/).

Generics and Traits.Generic Types and Functions:
**Generics** let you write code that works over many types, without duplicating it for each one.

## The problem generics solve

```rust
fn largest_i32(list: &[i32]) -> &i32 {
    let mut largest = &list[0];
    for item in list {
        if item > largest { largest = item; }
    }
    largest
}

fn largest_char(list: &[char]) -> &char {
    let mut largest = &list[0];
    for item in list {
        if item > largest { largest = item; }
    }
    largest
}
```

These two functions are identical except for the type. Generics let you write it once:

```rust
fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(list: &[T]) -> &T {
    let mut largest = &list[0];
    for item in list {
        if item > largest { largest = item; }
    }
    largest
}

fn main() {
    let numbers = vec![34, 50, 25, 100, 65];
    println!("{}", largest(&numbers)); // 100

    let chars = vec!['y', 'm', 'a', 'q'];
    println!("{}", largest(&chars)); // y
}
```

`<T: PartialOrd>` declares a generic type parameter `T`, constrained (via a **trait bound**, covered fully on the next page) to types that support ordering comparisons (`>`, `<`) — without that bound, the compiler would reject `item > largest`, since it can't assume an arbitrary type supports comparison.

## Generic structs

```rust
struct Point<T> {
    x: T,
    y: T,
}

fn main() {
    let integer_point = Point { x: 5, y: 10 };
    let float_point = Point { x: 1.0, y: 4.0 };
    // let mixed = Point { x: 5, y: 4.0 }; // ERROR: both fields must be the same T
}
```

Using two different generic parameters allows mixed types:

```rust
struct Point<T, U> {
    x: T,
    y: U,
}

let mixed = Point { x: 5, y: 4.0 }; // fine — T=i32, U=f64
```

## Generic enums

`Option<T>` and `Result<T, E>`, which you've already been using, are themselves generic enums:

```rust
enum Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None,
}

enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),
    Err(E),
}
```

## Generic methods

```rust
struct Point<T> {
    x: T,
    y: T,
}

impl<T> Point<T> {
    fn x(&self) -> &T {
        &self.x
    }
}

// methods can also apply only to specific concrete types:
impl Point<f64> {
    fn distance_from_origin(&self) -> f64 {
        (self.x.powi(2) + self.y.powi(2)).sqrt()
    }
}
```

`distance_from_origin` only exists on `Point<f64>`, not `Point<i32>` or any other `Point<T>` — this lets you provide type-specific behavior while keeping the struct itself fully generic.

## Zero runtime cost: monomorphization

A common worry: doesn't all this generic flexibility cost performance at runtime? No — Rust performs **monomorphization** at compile time: for every concrete type a generic function/struct is actually used with, the compiler generates a fully specialized copy, as if you'd hand-written `largest_i32` and `largest_char` separately. This means generics in Rust have **zero runtime overhead** compared to writing type-specific code by hand — the abstraction is entirely a compile-time convenience.

Next: [Traits and Trait Bounds](/rust/traits-and-trait-bounds/).

Generics and Traits.Traits and Trait Bounds:
A **trait** defines shared behavior — a set of methods a type promises to implement. It's Rust's answer to interfaces (Java/C#/TypeScript) or protocols (Swift), though with some important differences covered on this page.

## Defining and implementing a trait

```rust
trait Summary {
    fn summarize(&self) -> String;
}

struct Article {
    headline: String,
    author: String,
}

impl Summary for Article {
    fn summarize(&self) -> String {
        format!("{}, by {}", self.headline, self.author)
    }
}

struct Tweet {
    username: String,
    content: String,
}

impl Summary for Tweet {
    fn summarize(&self) -> String {
        format!("@{}: {}", self.username, self.content)
    }
}
```

Both `Article` and `Tweet` now implement `Summary`, each with its own logic — but any code that just needs "something summarizable" can work with either, through the trait, without caring which concrete type it actually is.

## Trait bounds: constraining generics

This is where traits and generics connect directly — a **trait bound** restricts a generic type parameter to only types that implement a given trait:

```rust
fn notify<T: Summary>(item: &T) {
    println!("Breaking news! {}", item.summarize());
}

// equivalent, more common shorthand for simple cases:
fn notify(item: &impl Summary) {
    println!("Breaking news! {}", item.summarize());
}
```

Both signatures mean the same thing: "accept a reference to *any* type, as long as it implements `Summary`." The `impl Trait` shorthand is more concise and is what you'll see most often in everyday code; the `<T: Trait>` generic form is needed when you have more complex requirements (multiple parameters that must be the *same* concrete type, or multiple bounds combined).

## Multiple trait bounds

```rust
use std::fmt::Display;

fn notify<T: Summary + Display>(item: &T) { /* ... */ }

// or with a `where` clause, more readable for complex bounds:
fn some_function<T, U>(t: &T, u: &U) -> i32
where
    T: Display + Clone,
    U: Clone + Summary,
{
    // ...
    0
}
```

## The orphan rule

One important restriction: you can only implement a trait for a type if **either the trait or the type is defined in your own crate**. You can't, for example, implement the standard library's `Display` trait for the standard library's `Vec<T>` from your own crate — both are "foreign," so this is disallowed. This rule (called the **orphan rule**, or "coherence") prevents two different crates from both providing conflicting implementations of the same foreign trait for the same foreign type, which would make it ambiguous which implementation to use.

You *can* implement your own trait for a foreign type, or a foreign trait for your own type — just not both foreign at once.

Next: [Default Implementations](/rust/default-implementations/).

Generics and Traits.Default Implementations:
A trait can provide **default method bodies** — implementations types can use as-is, or override if they need different behavior.

```rust
trait Summary {
    fn summarize_author(&self) -> String;

    fn summarize(&self) -> String {
        format!("(Read more from {}...)", self.summarize_author())
    }
}
```

Types implementing `Summary` are only *required* to provide `summarize_author`; `summarize` comes for free, built on top of it:

```rust
struct Tweet {
    username: String,
}

impl Summary for Tweet {
    fn summarize_author(&self) -> String {
        format!("@{}", self.username)
    }
    // summarize() is NOT implemented here — it uses the default
}

fn main() {
    let tweet = Tweet { username: String::from("rustlang") };
    println!("{}", tweet.summarize()); // "(Read more from @rustlang...)"
}
```

A type can still override the default if it needs to:

```rust
impl Summary for Tweet {
    fn summarize_author(&self) -> String {
        format!("@{}", self.username)
    }

    fn summarize(&self) -> String { // explicit override
        format!("Tweet from {}", self.summarize_author())
    }
}
```

## Why this pattern is useful

Default methods let a trait author provide genuinely useful shared logic once, while still requiring implementors to supply only the small, type-specific piece that logic depends on — this is exactly the pattern the standard library's `Iterator` trait uses: you implement just one method, `next()`, and get dozens of other methods (`map`, `filter`, `sum`, `collect`, and more, covered in [Iterators](/rust/iterators/)) for free, all built on top of `next()`.

```rust
trait Greet {
    fn name(&self) -> String;

    fn greet(&self) -> String {
        format!("Hello, {}!", self.name())
    }

    fn greet_loudly(&self) -> String {
        format!("{}!!!", self.greet().to_uppercase())
    }
}
```

Here, `greet_loudly` (a default method) even calls another default method, `greet` — default methods can call each other and call the required, non-default methods too, all through `self`.

Next: [Trait Objects and dyn](/rust/trait-objects-and-dyn/).

Generics and Traits.Trait Objects and dyn:
The generic `<T: Trait>` / `impl Trait` approach from earlier pages picks one concrete type *per call site* (resolved at compile time via monomorphization). Sometimes you genuinely need a single collection or variable that can hold **different concrete types at runtime**, as long as they all implement the same trait. That's what **trait objects** are for.

## The problem: heterogeneous collections

```rust
trait Draw {
    fn draw(&self);
}

struct Button;
impl Draw for Button {
    fn draw(&self) { println!("drawing a button"); }
}

struct Checkbox;
impl Draw for Checkbox {
    fn draw(&self) { println!("drawing a checkbox"); }
}
```

You can't put a `Button` and a `Checkbox` in the same `Vec<T>` for a single concrete `T` — they're different types. A trait object solves this:

```rust
fn main() {
    let components: Vec<Box<dyn Draw>> = vec![
        Box::new(Button),
        Box::new(Checkbox),
    ];

    for component in &components {
        component.draw();
    }
}
```

`Box<dyn Draw>` means "a heap-allocated value of *some* type that implements `Draw`, determined at runtime" — the `dyn` keyword marks it explicitly as a trait object, as opposed to a generic type parameter. Each element in the `Vec` can be a genuinely different concrete type underneath, as long as it implements `Draw`.

## Why Box is needed here

Rust needs to know the size of every type at compile time for most purposes, but different implementors of `Draw` (like `Button` and `Checkbox`) can have completely different sizes. `Box<T>` stores the value on the heap and keeps only a fixed-size pointer on the stack, sidestepping the size problem — this is covered further in the note on `Box` within [Common Derivable Traits](/rust/common-derivable-traits/) and its use for recursive types.

## Static dispatch (generics) vs dynamic dispatch (trait objects)

| | Generics (`impl Trait` / `<T: Trait>`) | Trait objects (`dyn Trait`) |
|---|---|---|
| Resolved | Compile time (monomorphization) | Runtime (a vtable lookup) |
| Performance | Zero cost, but larger binary (one copy per type used) | Small runtime cost per call, smaller binary |
| Can mix types in one collection? | No — one concrete `T` per call site | Yes — that's the whole point |
| Typical use | Function parameters, most everyday generic code | Plugin systems, UI component trees, heterogeneous collections |

As a rule of thumb: default to generics (`impl Trait`) — it's the more common, more idiomatic choice for typical function signatures. Reach for `dyn Trait` specifically when you need a genuinely mixed-type collection or a value whose concrete type can't be known until runtime.

## dyn without Box: as a function parameter

```rust
fn draw_all(components: &[Box<dyn Draw>]) {
    for component in components {
        component.draw();
    }
}

// or, borrowing a single trait object directly:
fn draw_one(component: &dyn Draw) {
    component.draw();
}
```

Next: [Operator Overloading](/rust/operator-overloading/).

Generics and Traits.Operator Overloading:
Rust lets you make your own types work with built-in operators (`+`, `-`, `==`, `[]`, and more) by implementing specific traits from `std::ops` and `std::cmp` — there's no special "operator overload" syntax; it's the exact same trait-implementation mechanism you've already seen.

## Overloading + with std::ops::Add

```rust
use std::ops::Add;

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
struct Point {
    x: i32,
    y: i32,
}

impl Add for Point {
    type Output = Point; // associated type: what + produces

    fn add(self, other: Point) -> Point {
        Point { x: self.x + other.x, y: self.y + other.y }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let p1 = Point { x: 1, y: 0 };
    let p2 = Point { x: 2, y: 3 };
    let p3 = p1 + p2; // calls Point::add — the compiler translates + into this

    println!("{p3:?}"); // Point { x: 3, y: 3 }
}
```

Writing `p1 + p2` is literally shorthand for `Add::add(p1, p2)` once `Point` implements `Add` — the compiler just wires the operator syntax to the trait method for you.

## Overloading == with PartialEq

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
struct Point { x: i32, y: i32 }

impl PartialEq for Point {
    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {
        self.x == other.x && self.y == other.y
    }
}

fn main() {
    let p1 = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };
    let p2 = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };
    println!("{}", p1 == p2); // true — calls PartialEq::eq
}
```

In practice, you'll almost always `#[derive(PartialEq)]` instead of writing this by hand (covered next page) — manual implementations are for cases where equality means something other than "all fields equal," which is fairly rare.

## Indexing with std::ops::Index

```rust
use std::ops::Index;

struct Grid {
    data: Vec<i32>,
    width: usize,
}

impl Index<(usize, usize)> for Grid {
    type Output = i32;

    fn index(&self, (row, col): (usize, usize)) -> &i32 {
        &self.data[row * self.width + col]
    }
}

fn main() {
    let grid = Grid { data: vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], width: 3 };
    println!("{}", grid[(1, 2)]); // 6 — calls Index::index
}
```

## Common operator traits at a glance

| Operator | Trait |
|---|---|
| `+` | `Add` |
| `-` | `Sub` |
| `*` | `Mul` |
| `/` | `Div` |
| `==`, `!=` | `PartialEq` |
| `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` | `PartialOrd` |
| `[]` | `Index` (and `IndexMut` for assignable indexing) |
| unary `-` | `Neg` |

