Collections Deep Dive.Vectors:
`Vec<T>` is Rust's growable, heap-allocated list — the collection you'll reach for by far the most often. (Fixed-size `[T; N]` arrays were covered briefly in [Scalar Types](/rust/scalar-types/); `Vec<T>` is what you want whenever the size isn't known upfront or needs to change.)

## Creating vectors

```rust
let v: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
let v = vec![1, 2, 3]; // the vec! macro, most common way to create one with initial values
let v: Vec<i32> = Vec::with_capacity(10); // pre-allocate space for 10 elements (an optimization)
```

## Adding and removing elements

```rust
let mut v = Vec::new();
v.push(5);
v.push(6);
v.push(7);

let last = v.pop(); // removes and returns the last element as Option<i32> -> Some(7)
v.insert(0, 100);     // insert at a specific index, shifting everything after it
v.remove(0);           // remove and return the element at a specific index
v.retain(|&x| x > 5);  // keep only elements matching a predicate
```

## Reading elements

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

let third = &v[2];               // panics if out of bounds
println!("{third}");

let third = v.get(2);            // returns Option<&i32> -> Some(&3), safe
match third {
    Some(value) => println!("{value}"),
    None => println!("no third element"),
}

println!("{}", v.len());          // 5
println!("{}", v.is_empty());     // false
println!("{}", v.contains(&3));   // true
```

**Indexing with `[]` panics on out-of-bounds access; `.get()` returns `Option` instead.** Prefer `.get()` when the index might be invalid (e.g. user input); use `[]` when you've already verified the index is valid (e.g. inside a loop over `0..v.len()`).

## Iterating

```rust
let v = vec![100, 32, 57];

for i in &v {              // immutable borrow of each element
    println!("{i}");
}

let mut v = vec![100, 32, 57];
for i in &mut v {           // mutable borrow — can modify in place
    *i += 50;                // dereference with * to modify the value itself
}

for i in v {                // by value — v is moved/consumed, unusable after
    println!("{i}");
}
```

## Storing different types via an enum

A `Vec<T>` requires every element to be the same type `T` — but `T` can be an enum, which lets you effectively store a fixed set of different "kinds" of data:

```rust
enum SpreadsheetCell {
    Int(i32),
    Float(f64),
    Text(String),
}

let row = vec![
    SpreadsheetCell::Int(3),
    SpreadsheetCell::Text(String::from("blue")),
    SpreadsheetCell::Float(10.12),
];
```

(For genuinely open-ended, unknown-at-compile-time types, that's what trait objects — `Vec<Box<dyn SomeTrait>>`, from [Trait Objects and dyn](/rust/trait-objects-and-dyn/) — are for instead.)

## Common patterns

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

let sum: i32 = v.iter().sum();
let doubled: Vec<i32> = v.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();
let evens: Vec<&i32> = v.iter().filter(|&&x| x % 2 == 0).collect();
let max = v.iter().max();     // Option<&i32>
let sorted = { let mut v2 = v.clone(); v2.sort(); v2 };
```

These `.iter()`-based patterns are extremely common — they're covered in full depth in [Iterators](/rust/iterators/).

