Collections Deep Dive.HashMap and HashSet:
## HashMap<K, V>

Rust's key-value store, equivalent to a Python `dict`, a JavaScript `Map`/object, or a Lua table used as a dictionary.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn main() {
    let mut scores = HashMap::new();
    scores.insert(String::from("Blue"), 10);
    scores.insert(String::from("Yellow"), 50);

    let team_name = String::from("Blue");
    let score = scores.get(&team_name); // Option<&i32> -> Some(&10)

    match score {
        Some(s) => println!("{s}"),
        None => println!("no such team"),
    }

    for (key, value) in &scores {
        println!("{key}: {value}");
    }
}
```

Unlike `Vec`, `HashMap` isn't in the prelude, so it needs an explicit `use std::collections::HashMap;`.

## Ownership with HashMap

Inserting a value **moves** it into the map (for non-`Copy` types like `String`):

```rust
let field_name = String::from("Favorite color");
let field_value = String::from("Blue");

let mut map = HashMap::new();
map.insert(field_name, field_value);
// field_name and field_value are no longer valid here — they were moved
```

## Updating values

```rust
let mut scores = HashMap::new();
scores.insert(String::from("Blue"), 10);

// overwrite unconditionally:
scores.insert(String::from("Blue"), 25);

// only insert if the key doesn't already exist:
scores.entry(String::from("Yellow")).or_insert(50);
scores.entry(String::from("Blue")).or_insert(50); // no-op, Blue already exists

// update based on the old value (a very common pattern — word counting, etc.):
let text = "hello world wonderful world";
let mut word_count = HashMap::new();
for word in text.split_whitespace() {
    let count = word_count.entry(word).or_insert(0);
    *count += 1;
}
println!("{word_count:?}"); // {"hello": 1, "world": 2, "wonderful": 1}
```

The `entry().or_insert()` pattern is idiomatic Rust's standard answer to "update if present, insert a default if not" — it does the lookup exactly once, rather than checking existence and then inserting/updating separately.

## HashSet<T>

A `HashSet` is effectively a `HashMap<T, ()>` — it stores unique values with no associated data, useful for membership checks and set operations:

```rust
use std::collections::HashSet;

fn main() {
    let mut fruits = HashSet::new();
    fruits.insert("apple");
    fruits.insert("banana");
    fruits.insert("apple"); // no-op, already present

    println!("{}", fruits.contains("apple")); // true
    println!("{}", fruits.len());              // 2

    let a: HashSet<i32> = [1, 2, 3].into_iter().collect();
    let b: HashSet<i32> = [2, 3, 4].into_iter().collect();

    let intersection: HashSet<_> = a.intersection(&b).collect();
    println!("{intersection:?}"); // {2, 3}
}
```

## Key requirement: Hash + Eq

Any type used as a `HashMap` key or `HashSet` element must implement `Hash` and `Eq` (covered in [Common Derivable Traits](/rust/common-derivable-traits/)) — most built-in types already do; for your own types, `#[derive(Hash, Eq, PartialEq)]` is usually all you need.

## No guaranteed order

`HashMap` and `HashSet` iteration order is **not guaranteed** and can even vary between runs of the same program (this is a deliberate security measure against a class of denial-of-service attack). If you need a predictable order, see the next page, [BTreeMap and Ordered Collections](/rust/btreemap-and-ordered-collections/).

Next: [BTreeMap and Ordered Collections](/rust/btreemap-and-ordered-collections/).

Collections Deep Dive.BTreeMap and Ordered Collections:
When you need key-value storage that maintains a **consistent, sorted order** (unlike `HashMap`, whose order is arbitrary and can even change between runs), reach for `BTreeMap`.

## BTreeMap<K, V>

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

fn main() {
    let mut scores = BTreeMap::new();
    scores.insert("Charlie", 92);
    scores.insert("Alice", 85);
    scores.insert("Bob", 78);

    for (name, score) in &scores {
        println!("{name}: {score}");
    }
    // always prints in key order: Alice, Bob, Charlie — guaranteed, every run
}
```

Same basic API as `HashMap` (`insert`, `get`, `entry().or_insert()`, iteration), but backed by a B-tree instead of a hash table, which keeps entries sorted by key at all times. The tradeoff: `BTreeMap` operations are `O(log n)` rather than `HashMap`'s average `O(1)` — slightly slower for lookups/inserts, in exchange for guaranteed order.

## When to use BTreeMap over HashMap

- You need to iterate in **sorted key order** (printing a report, generating consistent output, range queries).
- You need **range queries** — `BTreeMap` supports `.range(start..end)` to efficiently get a sorted sub-range of entries; `HashMap` has no equivalent.
- Your key type has a natural ordering, and that ordering actually matters to your use case (e.g. dates, version numbers, alphabetical names for display).

Otherwise, default to `HashMap` — it's faster for the common case of "just look things up by key," which is most of the time.

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

let mut events: BTreeMap<u32, &str> = BTreeMap::new();
events.insert(3, "third");
events.insert(1, "first");
events.insert(2, "second");

for (timestamp, event) in events.range(1..3) { // only keys 1 and 2
    println!("{timestamp}: {event}");
}
```

## BTreeSet<T>

The ordered counterpart to `HashSet`:

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

let mut tags: BTreeSet<&str> = BTreeSet::new();
tags.insert("rust");
tags.insert("backend");
tags.insert("api");

for tag in &tags {
    println!("{tag}"); // always prints alphabetically: api, backend, rust
}
```

