Collections Deep Dive.Iterators:
Iterators are one of Rust's most-used and most-loved features — a uniform, highly composable way to process sequences of values, with **zero runtime overhead** compared to writing the equivalent loop by hand.

## The Iterator trait

Every iterator implements one core method:

```rust
trait Iterator {
    type Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;
    // dozens of other methods (map, filter, sum, collect, ...) have DEFAULT
    // implementations built entirely on top of next() — see Default Implementations
}
```

Calling `.next()` repeatedly returns `Some(item)` until the sequence is exhausted, then `None` forever after.

## Creating iterators

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];

let iter1 = v.iter();       // yields &i32 (borrows each element)
let iter2 = v.into_iter();  // yields i32 (takes ownership, consumes v)
let mut v2 = vec![1, 2, 3];
let iter3 = v2.iter_mut();  // yields &mut i32 (allows modifying in place)
```

Iterators are **lazy** — creating one does nothing by itself; no work happens until you actually consume it (with a `for` loop, `.collect()`, `.sum()`, or similar):

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
let iter = v.iter().map(|x| {
    println!("processing {x}");
    x * 2
});
// nothing printed yet! the closure hasn't run.

let doubled: Vec<i32> = iter.collect(); // NOW it runs, printing for each element
```

## The essential adapter methods

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

let doubled: Vec<i32> = v.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();
let evens: Vec<&i32> = v.iter().filter(|&&x| x % 2 == 0).collect();
let sum: i32 = v.iter().sum();
let product: i32 = v.iter().product();
let count = v.iter().filter(|&&x| x > 3).count();
let max = v.iter().max();  // Option<&i32>
let min = v.iter().min();
let any_even = v.iter().any(|&x| x % 2 == 0);   // true
let all_even = v.iter().all(|&x| x % 2 == 0);    // false
let first_big = v.iter().find(|&&x| x > 4);       // Option<&i32> -> Some(&5)
let position = v.iter().position(|&x| x == 3);     // Option<usize> -> Some(2)
```

## Chaining multiple adapters

This is where iterators really shine — building a data-processing pipeline by chaining methods, evaluated lazily and efficiently in one pass:

```rust
let names = vec!["alice", "bob", "charlie", "dave"];

let result: Vec<String> = names
    .iter()
    .filter(|name| name.len() > 3)
    .map(|name| name.to_uppercase())
    .collect();

println!("{result:?}"); // ["ALICE", "CHARLIE", "DAVE"]
```

## enumerate, zip, rev, take, skip

```rust
let v = vec!["a", "b", "c"];

for (index, value) in v.iter().enumerate() {
    println!("{index}: {value}"); // 0: a, 1: b, 2: c
}

let a = vec![1, 2, 3];
let b = vec!["x", "y", "z"];
let zipped: Vec<(i32, &str)> = a.into_iter().zip(b.into_iter()).collect();
println!("{zipped:?}"); // [(1, "x"), (2, "y"), (3, "z")]

let last_two: Vec<&i32> = v.iter().rev().take(2).collect().into_iter().rev().collect();
let first_two: Vec<&&str> = v.iter().take(2).collect();
let skip_first: Vec<&&str> = v.iter().skip(1).collect();
```

## for loops vs iterator chains

Both are idiomatic Rust; which to prefer is largely about clarity for the task at hand:

```rust
// imperative style:
let mut total = 0;
for n in &v {
    if *n > 2 { total += n; }
}

// iterator-chain style — often preferred for simple transformations/aggregations:
let total: i32 = v.iter().filter(|&&n| n > 2).sum();
```

As a rule of thumb: a short chain of well-named adapter methods (`filter`, `map`, `sum`) is often more readable than an equivalent `for` loop with a manual accumulator, and is exactly as fast — the compiler optimizes iterator chains extremely aggressively, often compiling them down to code identical to a hand-written loop. Reach for an explicit `for` loop when the logic involves multiple unrelated side effects per iteration, or complex control flow (`break`/`continue` with labels) that doesn't map cleanly onto a chain.

This wraps up collections. Next: [String vs str](/rust/string-vs-str/).

I/O and Files.Reading Input:
## Reading a line from standard input

```rust
use std::io;

fn main() {
    println!("What's your name?");

    let mut input = String::new();
    io::stdin()
        .read_line(&mut input)
        .expect("failed to read line");

    let name = input.trim(); // remove the trailing newline
    println!("Hello, {name}!");
}
```

`read_line` **appends** to the given `String` (it doesn't clear it first) and returns `io::Result<usize>` (the number of bytes read) — this is why `.expect(...)` or proper error handling is typically attached. The input always includes a trailing newline character, so `.trim()` is almost always needed afterward.

## Reading and parsing a number

```rust
use std::io;

fn main() {
    println!("Enter a number:");

    let mut input = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).expect("failed to read line");

    let number: i32 = match input.trim().parse() {
        Ok(n) => n,
        Err(_) => {
            println!("That's not a valid number!");
            return;
        }
    };

    println!("You entered: {number}");
}
```

## A reusable input-reading loop

A common pattern for interactive CLI programs — keep asking until valid input is given:

```rust
use std::io::{self, Write};

fn read_number(prompt: &str) -> i32 {
    loop {
        print!("{prompt}");
        io::stdout().flush().unwrap(); // ensure the prompt appears before input is read

        let mut input = String::new();
        io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).expect("failed to read line");

        match input.trim().parse() {
            Ok(n) => return n,
            Err(_) => println!("Please enter a valid number."),
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let age = read_number("Enter your age: ");
    println!("You are {age} years old.");
}
```

`io::stdout().flush()` is needed here because `print!` (unlike `println!`) doesn't automatically flush its output buffer — without the explicit flush, the prompt might not appear on screen until *after* the program has already tried to read input, especially when output is piped or buffered.

## Reading all of stdin at once

```rust
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn main() {
    let mut buffer = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_to_string(&mut buffer).expect("failed to read stdin");
    println!("You entered {} bytes", buffer.len());
}
```

Useful for CLI tools designed to accept piped input, e.g. `cat file.txt | my_program`.

