Collections Deep Dive.Vectors:
`Vec<T>` is Rust's growable, heap-allocated list — the collection you'll reach for by far the most often. (Fixed-size `[T; N]` arrays were covered briefly in [Scalar Types](/rust/scalar-types/); `Vec<T>` is what you want whenever the size isn't known upfront or needs to change.)

## Creating vectors

```rust
let v: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
let v = vec![1, 2, 3]; // the vec! macro, most common way to create one with initial values
let v: Vec<i32> = Vec::with_capacity(10); // pre-allocate space for 10 elements (an optimization)
```

## Adding and removing elements

```rust
let mut v = Vec::new();
v.push(5);
v.push(6);
v.push(7);

let last = v.pop(); // removes and returns the last element as Option<i32> -> Some(7)
v.insert(0, 100);     // insert at a specific index, shifting everything after it
v.remove(0);           // remove and return the element at a specific index
v.retain(|&x| x > 5);  // keep only elements matching a predicate
```

## Reading elements

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

let third = &v[2];               // panics if out of bounds
println!("{third}");

let third = v.get(2);            // returns Option<&i32> -> Some(&3), safe
match third {
    Some(value) => println!("{value}"),
    None => println!("no third element"),
}

println!("{}", v.len());          // 5
println!("{}", v.is_empty());     // false
println!("{}", v.contains(&3));   // true
```

**Indexing with `[]` panics on out-of-bounds access; `.get()` returns `Option` instead.** Prefer `.get()` when the index might be invalid (e.g. user input); use `[]` when you've already verified the index is valid (e.g. inside a loop over `0..v.len()`).

## Iterating

```rust
let v = vec![100, 32, 57];

for i in &v {              // immutable borrow of each element
    println!("{i}");
}

let mut v = vec![100, 32, 57];
for i in &mut v {           // mutable borrow — can modify in place
    *i += 50;                // dereference with * to modify the value itself
}

for i in v {                // by value — v is moved/consumed, unusable after
    println!("{i}");
}
```

## Storing different types via an enum

A `Vec<T>` requires every element to be the same type `T` — but `T` can be an enum, which lets you effectively store a fixed set of different "kinds" of data:

```rust
enum SpreadsheetCell {
    Int(i32),
    Float(f64),
    Text(String),
}

let row = vec![
    SpreadsheetCell::Int(3),
    SpreadsheetCell::Text(String::from("blue")),
    SpreadsheetCell::Float(10.12),
];
```

(For genuinely open-ended, unknown-at-compile-time types, that's what trait objects — `Vec<Box<dyn SomeTrait>>`, from [Trait Objects and dyn](/rust/trait-objects-and-dyn/) — are for instead.)

## Common patterns

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

let sum: i32 = v.iter().sum();
let doubled: Vec<i32> = v.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();
let evens: Vec<&i32> = v.iter().filter(|&&x| x % 2 == 0).collect();
let max = v.iter().max();     // Option<&i32>
let sorted = { let mut v2 = v.clone(); v2.sort(); v2 };
```

These `.iter()`-based patterns are extremely common — they're covered in full depth in [Iterators](/rust/iterators/).

Next: [HashMap and HashSet](/rust/hashmap-and-hashset/).

Collections Deep Dive.HashMap and HashSet:
## HashMap<K, V>

Rust's key-value store, equivalent to a Python `dict`, a JavaScript `Map`/object, or a Lua table used as a dictionary.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn main() {
    let mut scores = HashMap::new();
    scores.insert(String::from("Blue"), 10);
    scores.insert(String::from("Yellow"), 50);

    let team_name = String::from("Blue");
    let score = scores.get(&team_name); // Option<&i32> -> Some(&10)

    match score {
        Some(s) => println!("{s}"),
        None => println!("no such team"),
    }

    for (key, value) in &scores {
        println!("{key}: {value}");
    }
}
```

Unlike `Vec`, `HashMap` isn't in the prelude, so it needs an explicit `use std::collections::HashMap;`.

## Ownership with HashMap

Inserting a value **moves** it into the map (for non-`Copy` types like `String`):

```rust
let field_name = String::from("Favorite color");
let field_value = String::from("Blue");

let mut map = HashMap::new();
map.insert(field_name, field_value);
// field_name and field_value are no longer valid here — they were moved
```

## Updating values

```rust
let mut scores = HashMap::new();
scores.insert(String::from("Blue"), 10);

// overwrite unconditionally:
scores.insert(String::from("Blue"), 25);

// only insert if the key doesn't already exist:
scores.entry(String::from("Yellow")).or_insert(50);
scores.entry(String::from("Blue")).or_insert(50); // no-op, Blue already exists

// update based on the old value (a very common pattern — word counting, etc.):
let text = "hello world wonderful world";
let mut word_count = HashMap::new();
for word in text.split_whitespace() {
    let count = word_count.entry(word).or_insert(0);
    *count += 1;
}
println!("{word_count:?}"); // {"hello": 1, "world": 2, "wonderful": 1}
```

The `entry().or_insert()` pattern is idiomatic Rust's standard answer to "update if present, insert a default if not" — it does the lookup exactly once, rather than checking existence and then inserting/updating separately.

## HashSet<T>

A `HashSet` is effectively a `HashMap<T, ()>` — it stores unique values with no associated data, useful for membership checks and set operations:

```rust
use std::collections::HashSet;

fn main() {
    let mut fruits = HashSet::new();
    fruits.insert("apple");
    fruits.insert("banana");
    fruits.insert("apple"); // no-op, already present

    println!("{}", fruits.contains("apple")); // true
    println!("{}", fruits.len());              // 2

    let a: HashSet<i32> = [1, 2, 3].into_iter().collect();
    let b: HashSet<i32> = [2, 3, 4].into_iter().collect();

    let intersection: HashSet<_> = a.intersection(&b).collect();
    println!("{intersection:?}"); // {2, 3}
}
```

## Key requirement: Hash + Eq

Any type used as a `HashMap` key or `HashSet` element must implement `Hash` and `Eq` (covered in [Common Derivable Traits](/rust/common-derivable-traits/)) — most built-in types already do; for your own types, `#[derive(Hash, Eq, PartialEq)]` is usually all you need.

## No guaranteed order

`HashMap` and `HashSet` iteration order is **not guaranteed** and can even vary between runs of the same program (this is a deliberate security measure against a class of denial-of-service attack). If you need a predictable order, see the next page, [BTreeMap and Ordered Collections](/rust/btreemap-and-ordered-collections/).

Next: [BTreeMap and Ordered Collections](/rust/btreemap-and-ordered-collections/).

Collections Deep Dive.BTreeMap and Ordered Collections:
When you need key-value storage that maintains a **consistent, sorted order** (unlike `HashMap`, whose order is arbitrary and can even change between runs), reach for `BTreeMap`.

## BTreeMap<K, V>

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

fn main() {
    let mut scores = BTreeMap::new();
    scores.insert("Charlie", 92);
    scores.insert("Alice", 85);
    scores.insert("Bob", 78);

    for (name, score) in &scores {
        println!("{name}: {score}");
    }
    // always prints in key order: Alice, Bob, Charlie — guaranteed, every run
}
```

Same basic API as `HashMap` (`insert`, `get`, `entry().or_insert()`, iteration), but backed by a B-tree instead of a hash table, which keeps entries sorted by key at all times. The tradeoff: `BTreeMap` operations are `O(log n)` rather than `HashMap`'s average `O(1)` — slightly slower for lookups/inserts, in exchange for guaranteed order.

## When to use BTreeMap over HashMap

- You need to iterate in **sorted key order** (printing a report, generating consistent output, range queries).
- You need **range queries** — `BTreeMap` supports `.range(start..end)` to efficiently get a sorted sub-range of entries; `HashMap` has no equivalent.
- Your key type has a natural ordering, and that ordering actually matters to your use case (e.g. dates, version numbers, alphabetical names for display).

Otherwise, default to `HashMap` — it's faster for the common case of "just look things up by key," which is most of the time.

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

let mut events: BTreeMap<u32, &str> = BTreeMap::new();
events.insert(3, "third");
events.insert(1, "first");
events.insert(2, "second");

for (timestamp, event) in events.range(1..3) { // only keys 1 and 2
    println!("{timestamp}: {event}");
}
```

## BTreeSet<T>

The ordered counterpart to `HashSet`:

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

let mut tags: BTreeSet<&str> = BTreeSet::new();
tags.insert("rust");
tags.insert("backend");
tags.insert("api");

for tag in &tags {
    println!("{tag}"); // always prints alphabetically: api, backend, rust
}
```

Next: [VecDeque and Other Collections](/rust/vecdeque-and-other-collections/).

Collections Deep Dive.VecDeque and Other Collections:
Beyond `Vec`, `HashMap`, and their ordered/set variants, the standard library provides a few more specialized collections worth knowing about.

## VecDeque<T> — a double-ended queue

`Vec` is efficient for adding/removing at the **end** (`push`/`pop`), but inserting/removing at the **front** requires shifting every other element — an `O(n)` operation. `VecDeque` (short for "double-ended queue") is efficient at both ends:

```rust
use std::collections::VecDeque;

fn main() {
    let mut queue: VecDeque<i32> = VecDeque::new();

    queue.push_back(1);   // add to the end — like Vec::push
    queue.push_back(2);
    queue.push_front(0);  // add to the front — O(1), unlike Vec::insert(0, ...)

    println!("{queue:?}"); // [0, 1, 2]

    let front = queue.pop_front(); // Some(0) — classic FIFO queue behavior
    let back = queue.pop_back();    // Some(2)
}
```

Use `VecDeque` when you need a **queue** (first-in-first-out processing) or need efficient insertion/removal at both ends. For a plain growable list where you only add/remove at the end, `Vec` remains the default choice — it's simpler and slightly faster for that common case.

## BinaryHeap<T> — a priority queue

Always gives you the **largest** element first, efficiently:

```rust
use std::collections::BinaryHeap;

fn main() {
    let mut heap = BinaryHeap::new();
    heap.push(3);
    heap.push(1);
    heap.push(4);
    heap.push(1);
    heap.push(5);

    while let Some(top) = heap.pop() {
        print!("{top} "); // 5 4 3 1 1 — always largest-first
    }
}
```

For a *min*-heap (smallest first), wrap values in `std::cmp::Reverse`:

```rust
use std::collections::BinaryHeap;
use std::cmp::Reverse;

let mut min_heap = BinaryHeap::new();
min_heap.push(Reverse(3));
min_heap.push(Reverse(1));
min_heap.push(Reverse(4));

while let Some(Reverse(top)) = min_heap.pop() {
    print!("{top} "); // 1 3 4 — smallest first
}
```

Useful for task scheduling, Dijkstra's algorithm, or any "always process the highest/lowest priority item next" logic.

## Quick decision guide

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| Growable list, mostly add/remove at the end | `Vec<T>` (default choice) |
| Growable list, need efficient front AND back operations | `VecDeque<T>` |
| Key-value lookup, order doesn't matter | `HashMap<K, V>` |
| Key-value lookup, need sorted order or range queries | `BTreeMap<K, V>` |
| Unique values, order doesn't matter | `HashSet<T>` |
| Unique values, need sorted order | `BTreeSet<T>` |
| Always need the max (or min) element next | `BinaryHeap<T>` |

